# Validating guest data

```elixir
Mix.install([{:kettlecorn, "~> 0.3.0"}])
```

## About this notebook

Validate guest structs before constructing host values. Start with one quote
constructor, then extend it to nested containers and ISO dates. Read
[Working with guest VMs](working-with-guests.livemd) first for the host/guest
boundary and returned values.

Requires Elixir 1.20 or later on x86_64 or ARM64 Linux or Apple silicon macOS.

```elixir
{:ok, runtime} = Kettlecorn.Install.runtime()
```

## Validate a quote

A guest struct whose module atom exists on the host arrives as
`Kettlecorn.Struct`, holding its module and fields as data. Otherwise it stays
a map with `__struct__: {:unknown_atom, name}`. Rebuilding a host struct enables
that module's protocol implementations. Accept only known modules and validate
their fields before construction. Start with a single quote type:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Quote do
  @enforce_keys [:sku, :price]
  defstruct [:sku, :price]

  def new(sku, price) when is_binary(sku) and is_number(price) and price >= 0,
    do: {:ok, %__MODULE__{sku: sku, price: price}}

  def new(_sku, _price), do: {:error, :invalid_quote}

  def from_guest(%Kettlecorn.Struct{module: __MODULE__, fields: %{sku: sku, price: price}}),
    do: new(sku, price)

  def from_guest(_other), do: {:error, :invalid_quote}
end
```

A pricing plugin defines a module of the same name in the guest (in a
cooked application, both sides would compile the same file) and returns
quotes:

```elixir
plugin = ~S"""
defmodule MyApp.Quote do
  defstruct [:sku, :price]
end

defmodule Pricing do
  def quote("apple"), do: {:ok, %MyApp.Quote{sku: "apple", price: 0.5}}
  def quote("pear"), do: {:ok, %MyApp.Quote{sku: "pear", price: -1}}
  def quote(sku), do: {:ok, URI.parse("https://example.com/" <> sku)}
end
"""

{:ok, pricing} =
  Kettlecorn.start_link(
    max_memory: 128 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_fuel: 1_000_000_000_000,
    max_lifetime: 600_000
  )

{:ok, _} = Kettlecorn.eval(pricing, plugin)

{:ok, %{"value" => apple}} = Kettlecorn.eval(pricing, ~S|Pricing.quote("apple")|)
apple
```

`from_guest/1` accepts the expected module and validates its fields:

```elixir
{:ok, quote} = apple
{:ok, %MyApp.Quote{sku: "apple", price: 0.5}} = MyApp.Quote.from_guest(quote)
```

A negative price is rejected by the same constructor:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => {:ok, quote}}} = Kettlecorn.eval(pricing, ~S|Pricing.quote("pear")|)
{:error, :invalid_quote} = MyApp.Quote.from_guest(quote)
```

## Convert nested values

A plugin can return quotes inside a larger result, such as a dated price list:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => catalog}} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(pricing, ~S|%{"date" => ~D[2026-01-01], "quotes" => [Pricing.quote("apple")]}|)

%{
  "date" => %Kettlecorn.Struct{module: Date},
  "quotes" => [{:ok, %Kettlecorn.Struct{module: MyApp.Quote}}]
} =
  catalog
```

Validating only the outer map would leave its guest structs unconverted. Traverse
containers and apply a constructor to each accepted struct. The converter below
accepts quotes and ISO dates, rejects other `Kettlecorn.Struct` values, and
preserves ordinary maps, guest pid handles and
opaque handles as data. The preceding VM has a ten-minute lifetime; restart
the quote example if it expires.

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.GuestData do
  def cast(%Kettlecorn.Struct{module: MyApp.Quote, fields: %{sku: sku, price: price}}),
    do: MyApp.Quote.new(sku, price)

  # Date's calendar is a module whose callbacks run on the host; only accept ISO.
  def cast(%Kettlecorn.Struct{
        module: Date,
        fields: %{calendar: Calendar.ISO, year: year, month: month, day: day}
      })
      when is_integer(year) and is_integer(month) and is_integer(day),
      do: Date.new(year, month, day)

  def cast(%Kettlecorn.Struct{module: Date}), do: {:error, :invalid_date}

  def cast(%Kettlecorn.Struct{module: module}), do: {:error, {:unexpected_struct, module}}

  def cast(%handle{} = value) when handle in [Kettlecorn.Pid, Kettlecorn.Opaque], do: {:ok, value}

  def cast(list) when is_list(list), do: cast_list(list, [])

  def cast(tuple) when is_tuple(tuple) do
    with {:ok, list} <- cast_list(Tuple.to_list(tuple), []), do: {:ok, List.to_tuple(list)}
  end

  def cast(map) when is_map(map) do
    with {:ok, pairs} <- cast_list(Map.to_list(map), []), do: {:ok, Map.new(pairs)}
  end

  def cast(other), do: {:ok, other}

  defp cast_list([head | tail], acc) do
    with {:ok, head} <- cast(head), do: cast_list(tail, [head | acc])
  end

  defp cast_list([], acc), do: {:ok, Enum.reverse(acc)}

  defp cast_list(tail, acc) do
    with {:ok, tail} <- cast(tail), do: {:ok, :lists.reverse(acc, tail)}
  end
end
```

The nested result becomes a host date and a quote validated by its constructor:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"date" => ~D[2026-01-01], "quotes" => [{:ok, %MyApp.Quote{sku: "apple", price: 0.5}}]}} =
  MyApp.GuestData.cast(catalog)
```

ISO dates are reconstructed from integer fields. Missing fields, wrong
types and other calendars are refused before calling `Date.new/3`:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => date}} = Kettlecorn.eval(pricing, "~D[2026-01-01]")
{:ok, ~D[2026-01-01]} = MyApp.GuestData.cast(date)

for fields <- [
      %{calendar: Calendar.ISO},
      %{calendar: Calendar.ISO, year: "2026", month: 1, day: 1},
      %{calendar: MyApp.Calendar, year: 2026, month: 1, day: 1}
    ] do
  {:error, :invalid_date} = MyApp.GuestData.cast(%Kettlecorn.Struct{module: Date, fields: fields})
end
```

`MyApp.Quote.new/2` rejects a quote with an invalid price. `cast/1` rejects
structs the application doesn't accept:

```elixir
for sku <- ["pear", "plum"] do
  {:ok, %{"value" => value}} = Kettlecorn.eval(pricing, "Pricing.quote(#{inspect(sku)})")
  {sku, MyApp.GuestData.cast(value)}
end
```

```elixir
Kettlecorn.stop(pricing)
```

`struct!/2` checks keys but does not validate field values. Validate those
values before constructing host structs or running their protocol implementations.

## Convert guest values for JSON

`Kettlecorn.ETF.to_json/1` converts decoded values to data that
`JSON.encode!/1` accepts. Convert the apple quote returned by the VM
earlier in the notebook:

```elixir
{:ok, ["ok", %{"__struct__" => "Elixir.MyApp.Quote", "sku" => "apple", "price" => 0.5}] = quote_json} =
  Kettlecorn.ETF.to_json(apple)

JSON.encode!(quote_json)
```

The conversion rejects map keys that become the same string:

```elixir
{:error, {:duplicate_key, "quote"}} = Kettlecorn.ETF.to_json(%{:quote => 1, "quote" => 2})
```

